    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. i'm a bit worried about the LVT though. i've heard that in high-traffic areas it can end up looking pretty beat up with all the scuffs and micro-scratches from people walking on it every day. is there anything specific about the way LVT wears down that we should watch out for? also how does the stone compare in terms of actual surface damage over 5-10 years?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right to be cautious about LVT in a high-traffic setting; it can indeed suffer from surface wear, which is a very real concern, especially with the sheer number of footfalls in a busy lobby. The wear on LVT often manifests as surface abrasion, where the top layer of the vinyl gets dulled or scratched by grit and heavy shoes. In comparison, natural stone, while it can chip or crack if subjected to a heavy impact, tends to resist general surface scratching better than LVT, though it requires careful sealing to prevent etching from acidic spills.\n\nIf you want to mitigate the LVT issue, look for high-quality, commercial-grade LVT with a very thick wear layer. However, even the best LVT will show signs of use over a decade, whereas the stone's primary long-term risk is chemical damage or impact damage, which is usually more visible than simple scuffing. For a truly bulletproof surface, polished concrete, if properly sealed and maintained, often proves to be the most resilient option against daily abrasion.",
      "source": "target",
